\begin{abstract}
We study the \emph{type channel} of a real cyclotomic field: the map sending a
prime $p$, unramified in $\Q(\zeta_f)$, to its residue degree in the maximal real
subfield $\Q(\zeta_f)^{+}$. At conductor $f = 56$ this channel sits at the first
rung of the abelian ladder whose Galois group has composite order and is not
cyclic. We establish: (i) $[\Q(\zetaf):\Q] = 24$, the involution attached to $-1$
has order $2$, and its fixed field $\Q(\zetaf)^{+}$ has degree exactly $12$;
(ii) an explicit isomorphism $\Gal(\Q(\zetaf)^{+}/\Q)\cong C_6\times C_2$ built
from the basis $\{3,13,-1\}$ of $(\Z/56)^\times\cong C_6\times C_2\times C_2$, so
the group is abelian of order $12$ and non-cyclic; (iii) the residue degree of a
prime is the order of its Frobenius class, with type densities
$\{1/12,1/4,1/6,1/2\}$ matching the order statistics of $C_6\times C_2$;
(iv) \emph{full pinning} as a theorem about deterministic channels, with the exact
value $H(T) = \tfrac43 + \tfrac14\log_2 3 = 1.72957\ldots$ bits, rigorously
bracketed by $1.7295 < H(T) < 1.7296$, and strictly positive residual uncertainty
$H(X\mid T) = \tfrac53 + \tfrac34\log_2 3$; (v) orbit purity in finite abelian
groups, giving $f\cdot g = 12$ with the four shapes $(1,12),(2,6),(3,4),(6,2)$;
(vi) a translation-invariant pair law showing a semiprime carries exactly the
prime type profile; and (vii) \emph{entropic separation}: $H(C_{12}) = \tfrac56 +
\log_2 3 > 2 > \tfrac43 + \tfrac14\log_2 3 = H(C_6\times C_2)$, so the single
measured number $1.7296$ certifies non-cyclicity of the Galois group.
\end{abstract}

\noindent\textbf{Keywords:} real cyclotomic field, residue degree, Frobenius
class, Chebotarev density, Shannon entropy, mutual information, deterministic
channel, abelian Galois group, order profile.

\section{Introduction}

\subsection{The abelian ladder and the problem of the rung}

Let $f\ge 3$ and $\zeta_f = e^{2\pi i/f}$. The cyclotomic field $\Q(\zeta_f)$ is
abelian over $\Q$ with Galois group canonically $(\Z/f)^\times$, via the
cyclotomic character $\sigma\mapsto a_\sigma$ determined by $\sigma(\zeta_f) =
\zeta_f^{a_\sigma}$. The maximal real subfield
\[
  \Q(\zeta_f)^{+} \;=\; \Q\!\left(\zeta_f + \zeta_f^{-1}\right)
\]
is the fixed field of complex conjugation, the automorphism attached to $-1$; its
Galois group is $G^{+} = (\Z/f)^\times/\{\pm 1\}$, of order $\varphi(f)/2$ for
$f>2$.

Consider the ladder of these real fields indexed by degree $n = \varphi(f)/2$.
Degree $12$ is the first place where an abelian group of that order need not be
cyclic: the abelian groups of order $12$ are $C_{12}$ and $C_6\times C_2$, and
both occur as $G^{+}$ for suitable conductors. We ask whether an intrinsic,
measurable quantity---computable from prime data alone---determines which occurs.
The answer developed here is a single real number: the Shannon entropy of the
residue-degree distribution. At conductor $56$ it equals
$\tfrac43+\tfrac14\log_2 3 = 1.7296\ldots$ bits, a value the cyclic group cannot
produce (Theorem~\ref{thm:sep}).

\subsection{Why conductor $56$}

Exactly ten conductors satisfy $\varphi(f) = 24$, namely
$f\in\{35,39,45,52,56,70,72,78,84,90\}$, of which $70,78,90$ define the same
fields as $35,39,45$ (since $\Q(\zeta_{2m}) = \Q(\zeta_m)$ for odd $m$). Among the
seven distinct fields, four have cyclic $G^{+}$ ($f = 35,39,45,52$) and three have
$G^{+}\cong C_6\times C_2$ ($f = 56,72,84$). Conductor $56$ is the smallest with
non-cyclic $G^{+}$. Structurally: $56 = 2^3\cdot 7$, so
$(\Z/56)^\times\cong(\Z/8)^\times\times(\Z/7)^\times\cong (C_2\times C_2)\times
C_6$, and quotienting the $\{\pm1\}$ factor leaves $C_2\times C_6$, of exponent
$6 < 12$.

\section{Notation and basic objects}

Throughout $\log_2$ is the binary logarithm and entropies are in bits.

\begin{definition}
Let $U_{56} = \{a\in\Z/56 : \gcd(a,56) = 1\}$ be the set of reduced residue
classes.
\end{definition}

\begin{lemma}[Three equivalent descriptions]\label{lem:units}
For $a\in\Z/56$ the following are equivalent: (i) $a\in U_{56}$; (ii) $ab = 1$ for
some $b$; (iii) $a^6 = 1$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(i)$\Leftrightarrow$(ii) is the standard description of units in $\Z/m$. For
(iii): by the Chinese Remainder Theorem $(\Z/56)^\times \cong
(\Z/8)^\times\times(\Z/7)^\times$ has exponent $\lcm(2,6) = 6$, so $a^6 = 1$ for
every unit; conversely $a^6 = 1$ gives $a\cdot a^5 = 1$. Both directions are
finite checks over the $56$ residues.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$|U_{56}| = \varphi(56) = 24$ and $U_{56}$ has exponent $6$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{lemma}
If $p$ is prime and $p\notin\{2,7\}$ then $p\bmod 56\in U_{56}$; the excluded
primes are exactly the ramified ones.
\end{lemma}

\begin{definition}[Type / residue degree]
For $a\in U_{56}$ set
\[
  T(a) \;=\; \min\{k\ge 1 : a^k = 1 \text{ or } a^k = -1\},
\]
equivalently the order of the class of $a$ in $G^{+} = U_{56}/\{\pm1\}$.
\end{definition}

Since the exponent is $6$, we have $T(a)\mid 6$, and $T$ is computed by the
cascade
\[
  T(a) = \begin{cases}
    1, & a = \pm1,\\
    2, & a^2 = \pm 1,\ a\ne\pm1,\\
    3, & a^3 = \pm1 \text{ and neither of the above},\\
    6, & \text{otherwise.}
  \end{cases}
\]

\begin{lemma}[Basic properties]\label{lem:T}
\begin{enumerate}
\item[(a)] $a^{T(a)} = \pm1$, and no $1\le k < T(a)$ satisfies $a^k = \pm1$.
\item[(b)] $T(-a) = T(a)$, so $T$ descends to $G^{+}$.
\item[(c)] $T(a)\in\{1,2,3,6\}$ for all $a\in U_{56}$.
\item[(d)] (Pinning hypothesis) If $p\equiv q \pmod{56}$ then $T(p) = T(q)$.
\end{enumerate}
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
(a)--(c) are finite verifications over the $24$ residues; (b) because
$(-a)^k = \pm a^k$; (d) because $T$ is a function on $\Z/56$.
\end{proof}

Property (d) is the whole arithmetic input to the information-theoretic story:
the type of a prime is a deterministic function of its remainder. In general this
is the statement of class field theory that splitting in an abelian extension of
conductor $f$ depends only on $p\bmod f$.

\section{Structure of the unit group and of $G^{+}$}

\begin{definition}
Define $\beta : C_6\times C_2\times C_2 \to \Z/56$ by
$\beta(i,j,k) = 3^{i}\,13^{j}\,(-1)^{k}$, with $i$ read mod $6$ and $j,k$ mod $2$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Basis theorem]\label{thm:basis}
$\beta$ is injective with image exactly $U_{56}$ and satisfies
$\beta(t+s) = \beta(t)\beta(s)$. Hence
$(\Z/56)^\times \cong C_6\times C_2\times C_2$, the factors being generated by
$3$ (order $6$), $13$ (order $2$) and $-1$ (order $2$).
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$3^6 = 729 = 13\cdot 56+1$ and no proper divisor of $6$ works, so $\ord(3) = 6$;
$13^2 = 169 = 3\cdot56+1$; $(-1)^2 = 1$. Homomorphy is commutativity.
Injectivity is a finite check on $24$ triples, and $|U_{56}| = 24$ then forces
surjectivity. Structurally this is the Chinese Remainder decomposition written in
a basis adapted to $\{\pm1\}$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
Write $c : C_6\times C_2\to U_{56}$, $c(i,j) = 3^{i}13^{j}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{proposition}[Splitting of $G^{+}$]\label{prop:split}
$c$ is a homomorphism from $(C_6\times C_2,+)$ to $(U_{56},\cdot)$ with $c(0)=1$;
it is injective modulo signs (if $c(g) = \pm c(h)$ then $g = h$); and every
$a\in U_{56}$ equals $c(g)$ or $-c(g)$ for a unique $g$. Consequently the twelve
classes $\{\pm c(g)\}$ partition $U_{56}$ and
$G^{+} = U_{56}/\{\pm1\}\cong C_6\times C_2$ has order $12$.
\end{proposition}

\begin{theorem}[Degree twelve]\label{thm:deg12}
Let $L = \Q(\zetaf)$ and let $\iota\in\Gal(L/\Q)$ correspond to $-1$ under the
cyclotomic character. Then $[L:\Q] = \varphi(56) = 24$, $\ord(\iota) = 2$, and the
fixed field $\Q(\zetaf)^{+} = L^{\langle\iota\rangle}$ satisfies
$[L:\Q(\zetaf)^{+}] = 2$ and
\[
  \big[\Q(\zetaf)^{+}:\Q\big] \;=\; 12 .
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The $56$-th cyclotomic polynomial is irreducible over $\Q$ of degree $24$; the
cyclotomic character is an isomorphism onto $(\Z/56)^\times$, where $-1$ has order
$2$. Since $L/\Q$ is Galois, Artin's theorem gives
$[L:L^{\langle\iota\rangle}] = |\langle\iota\rangle| = 2$, and multiplicativity of
degrees in the tower $\Q\subseteq\Q(\zetaf)^{+}\subseteq L$ yields $12\cdot 2 =
24$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Galois group of the real field]\label{thm:gal}
There is an isomorphism
\[
  \Gal\big(\Q(\zetaf)^{+}/\Q\big) \;\cong\; (\Z/56)^\times/\{\pm1\}
  \;\cong\; C_6\times C_2 .
\]
In particular the group has order $12$ and is not cyclic.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
$\langle\iota\rangle$ is normal since the ambient group is abelian, so the Galois
correspondence gives $\Gal(\Q(\zetaf)^{+}/\Q)\cong\Gal(L/\Q)/\langle\iota\rangle$;
transporting along the cyclotomic character and applying
Proposition~\ref{prop:split} identifies this with $C_6\times C_2$. Every
$g\in C_6\times C_2$ satisfies $6g = 0$, so the exponent divides $6 < 12$ and no
element generates.
\end{proof}

\section{Types are Frobenius orders}

\begin{theorem}[Type $=$ order]\label{thm:typeord}
For every $g\in C_6\times C_2$ we have $\ord(g) = T(c(g))$. Under
$G^{+}\cong C_6\times C_2$, the residue degree of an unramified prime in
$\Q(\zetaf)^{+}$ is the order of its Frobenius class.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Put $m = T(c(g))$. Then $c(mg) = c(g)^m = \pm 1 = \pm c(0)$, so $mg = 0$ by
Proposition~\ref{prop:split}. If $0<k<m$ had $kg = 0$ then $c(g)^k = 1$,
contradicting minimality. Hence $m = \ord(g)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{definition}
For $d\ge1$ set $N(d) = \#\{a\in U_{56} : T(a) = d\}$ and
$M(d) = \#\{g\in C_6\times C_2 : \ord(g) = d\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Chebotarev match]\label{thm:cheb}
$N(d) = 2M(d)$ for all $d$. Explicitly
\[
  (N(1),N(2),N(3),N(6)) = (2,6,4,12),\qquad
  (M(1),M(2),M(3),M(6)) = (1,3,2,6),
\]
all other values being $0$, with $N(1)+N(2)+N(3)+N(6) = 24$. The type densities
are $\left(\tfrac1{12},\tfrac14,\tfrac16,\tfrac12\right)$, exactly the order
statistics of $C_6\times C_2$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Each $g$ lifts to exactly two residues $\pm c(g)$, of equal type
(Lemma~\ref{lem:T}(b)) equal to $\ord(g)$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:typeord}); so the
fibres of $T$ double those of $\ord$. For $d\notin\{1,2,3,6\}$ both sides vanish
since every order divides the exponent $6$. The counts are the finite enumeration
of $C_6\times C_2$: the identity; three involutions $(3,0),(0,1),(3,1)$; two
elements of order $3$, namely $(2,0),(4,0)$; six of order $6$.
\end{proof}

\begin{remark}
Because $T(p)$ depends only on $p\bmod 56$ and the Frobenius class of $p$ is the
class of $p\bmod56$, Chebotarev's theorem (here, Dirichlet's theorem on primes in
progressions) gives these as the natural densities of primes of each type.
Empirically, over the $17\,982$ primes below $200\,000$ other than $2$ and $7$,
the observed frequencies are $0.08247, 0.24992, 0.16466, 0.50295$ against the
predicted $0.08333, 0.25, 0.16667, 0.5$.
\end{remark}

\section{A finite information calculus and full pinning}

Let $S$ be a nonempty finite set, $\varphi : S\to\mathcal{T}$ any function into a
finite symbol set, and $X$ uniform on $S$.

\begin{definition}
With the convention $0\log_2 0 = 0$, and $q(a,t) = |S|^{-1}$ when
$\varphi(a) = t$, $q(a,t) = 0$ otherwise,
\[
  H(X) = \log_2|S|,\quad
  H(T) = \!\!\sum_{t\in\varphi(S)}\!\! -\Pr[T{=}t]\log_2\Pr[T{=}t],\quad
  H(X,T) = \!\!\!\sum_{(a,t)\in S\times\varphi(S)}\!\!\! -q(a,t)\log_2 q(a,t),
\]
\[
  I(X;T) = H(X)+H(T)-H(X,T),\qquad H(X\mid T) = H(X,T)-H(T).
\]
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Determinism collapses the joint entropy]\label{thm:joint}
$H(X,T) = H(X) = \log_2|S|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For fixed $a$, exactly one term of the inner sum is nonzero, at $t = \varphi(a)$,
contributing $|S|^{-1}\log_2|S|$; summing over the $|S|$ elements gives
$\log_2|S|$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Full pinning]\label{thm:pin}
For every nonempty finite $S$ and every $\varphi$,
$I(X;\varphi(X)) = H(\varphi(X))$; the pinning gap $H(T)-I(X;T)$ vanishes
identically.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Substitute Theorem~\ref{thm:joint} into $I = H(X)+H(T)-H(X,T)$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}
$H(X\mid T) = \log_2|S| - H(T)$.
\end{corollary}

Theorem~\ref{thm:pin} says a channel is fully pinned if and only if it is
deterministic. The substantive content of ``$I(p\bmod56;T) = H(T)$ with gap $0$''
is therefore the arithmetic fact of Lemma~\ref{lem:T}(d); the entropy calculus
converts it into an exact bit count.

\section{The exact entropy at conductor $56$}

\begin{theorem}[Closed form]\label{thm:closed}
With $S = U_{56}$ and $\varphi = T$,
\[
  H(T) = \tfrac1{12}\log_2 12 + \tfrac14\log_2 4 + \tfrac16\log_2 6 +
  \tfrac12\log_2 2 = \frac43 + \frac{\log_2 3}{4}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The fibre sizes are $2,6,4,12$ out of $24$ (Theorem~\ref{thm:cheb}), i.e.\
probabilities $\tfrac1{12},\tfrac14,\tfrac16,\tfrac12$; a Shannon term of
probability $1/x$ contributes $\tfrac1x\log_2 x$. Expanding
$\log_212 = 2+\log_23$, $\log_26 = 1+\log_23$, $\log_24 = 2$, $\log_22 = 1$ and
collecting gives $\tfrac43+\tfrac14\log_23$.
\end{proof}

\begin{lemma}[Rigorous bracketing]\label{lem:bracket}
$\dfrac{84}{53} < \log_2 3 < \dfrac{233}{147}$.
\end{lemma}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
The integer inequalities $2^{84} < 3^{53}$ and $3^{147} < 2^{233}$ are finite
computations; take logarithms and divide by $53\log 2$, resp.\ $147\log 2$. These
are continued-fraction convergents of $\log_23 = 1.5849625\ldots$
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Numerical value]
$1.7295 < H(T) < 1.7296$; the true value is $1.7295739\ldots$ bits.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Full pinning at conductor $56$]
$I(p\bmod 56\,;T) = H(T) = \tfrac43+\tfrac14\log_23$ and $H(T)-I = 0$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{theorem}[Fully pinned but strictly lossy]
$H(X) = \log_2 24 = 3+\log_23 \approx 4.58496$ and
$H(X\mid T) = \tfrac53+\tfrac34\log_23 \approx 2.85539 > 0$.
\end{theorem}

Thus the type map is a deterministic coarsening: it leaks nothing, yet retains
only $1.73$ of the $4.58$ bits carried by the residue, an efficiency of about
$37.7\%$.

\section{Entropic separation}

\begin{definition}[Order-profile entropy]
For a finite group $A$ put
$H(A) = -\sum_d \frac{M_A(d)}{|A|}\log_2\frac{M_A(d)}{|A|}$ where
$M_A(d) = \#\{x\in A : \ord(x) = d\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[The two order-$12$ profiles]\label{thm:profiles}
\[
  H(C_{12}) = \frac56 + \log_2 3 = 2.41830\ldots, \qquad
  H(C_6\times C_2) = \frac43 + \frac{\log_2 3}{4} = 1.72957\ldots
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
In $C_{12}$ the orders $1,2,3,4,6,12$ occur with multiplicities $\varphi(d) =
1,1,2,2,2,4$, i.e.\ probabilities
$\tfrac1{12},\tfrac1{12},\tfrac16,\tfrac16,\tfrac16,\tfrac13$; summing gives
$2\cdot\tfrac1{12}\log_212 + 3\cdot\tfrac16\log_26 + \tfrac13\log_23 =
\tfrac56+\log_23$. For $C_6\times C_2$ the orders $1,2,3,6$ occur with
multiplicities $1,3,2,6$, and the computation is Theorem~\ref{thm:closed}.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Entropy separation at order twelve]\label{thm:sep}
$H(C_{12}) > 2 > H(C_6\times C_2)$; in particular the two values differ, so the
order-profile entropy is a complete isomorphism invariant among abelian groups of
order $12$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
By Lemma~\ref{lem:bracket}, $\tfrac56+\log_23 > \tfrac56+\tfrac{84}{53} =
2.4182\ldots > 2$ and $\tfrac43+\tfrac14\cdot\tfrac{233}{147} = 1.72959\ldots < 2$.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[Entropic certificate of non-cyclicity]
An abelian group of order $12$ with order-profile entropy below $2$ bits is not
isomorphic to $C_{12}$, hence is isomorphic to $C_6\times C_2$.
\end{corollary}

\begin{theorem}[Channel transfer]
$H(T) = H(C_6\times C_2) = \tfrac43+\tfrac14\log_23$: the arithmetic channel on
residues and the group-theoretic order profile carry the same information.
\end{theorem}

Measuring the type frequencies of primes, computing their entropy, obtaining
$1.7296 < 2$, and applying the corollary reproduces Theorem~\ref{thm:gal} by a
completely different route.

\section{Orbit purity and the decomposition law}

Let $A$ be a finite abelian group (written additively) and $g\in A$.

\begin{definition}
The translation orbit of $x$ under $g$ is
$\mathcal{O}_g(x) = \{kg + x : 0\le k < \ord(g)\}$.
\end{definition}

\begin{theorem}[Orbit purity]\label{thm:orbit}
$|\mathcal{O}_g(x)| = \ord(g)$ for every $x$; distinct orbits are disjoint; and
$(\#\text{orbits})\cdot\ord(g) = |A|$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
Injectivity of $k\mapsto kg+x$ on $0\le k<\ord(g)$ is exactly the statement that
$kg = k'g$ forces $k\equiv k'$ mod $\ord(g)$. Each orbit is a coset of
$\langle g\rangle$, so membership determines the orbit and the orbits partition
$A$ into blocks of equal size; a fibrewise count gives the product formula.
\end{proof}

\begin{corollary}[The $efg$ law at conductor $56$]
For $p\ne 2,7$, with $f = T(p)$ the residue degree and $g$ the number of primes
above $p$ in $\Q(\zetaf)^{+}$,
\[
  e\cdot f\cdot g \;=\; 1\cdot f\cdot g \;=\; 12,\qquad
  (f,g)\in\{(1,12),(2,6),(3,4),(6,2)\}.
\]
All orbits have the same length: there are no short or mixed orbits.
\end{corollary}

\section{The semiprime pair channel}

\begin{theorem}[Translation-invariant pair law]\label{thm:pair}
Let $S$ be a finite subset of a monoid such that for each $u\in S$ the map
$v\mapsto uv$ is injective and maps $S$ onto $S$. Then for any $\varphi$ on $S$
and any $t$,
\[
  \#\{(u,v)\in S\times S : \varphi(uv) = t\} \;=\; |S|\cdot\#\{w\in S:\varphi(w)=t\}.
\]
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}[Proof sketch]
For fixed $u$ the map $v\mapsto uv$ is a bijection of $S$, so the inner count
equals $\#\{w:\varphi(w)=t\}$ independently of $u$; sum over the $|S|$ choices of
$u$.
\end{proof}

\begin{theorem}[Semiprime channel at conductor $56$]
$U_{56}$ is a group, so for every $d$,
$\#\{(u,v)\in U_{56}^2 : T(uv)=d\} = 24\,N(d)$, explicitly $48,144,96,288$ for
$d = 1,2,3,6$ out of $576$ ordered pairs. Dividing by $576$ recovers the densities
$\tfrac1{12},\tfrac14,\tfrac16,\tfrac12$, so a semiprime transmits exactly
$H(T) = \tfrac43+\tfrac14\log_23 = 1.72957\ldots$ bits.
\end{theorem}

The law is exact enumeration, with no error term; and it is not a triviality,
since the pair channel has a much larger input space ($576$ versus $24$) yet an
identical output distribution.

\section{Algorithms}

\paragraph{Type computation.} Given $m$ and $a$ coprime to $m$, iterate
$x\leftarrow a,a^2,\dots$ modulo $m$ and stop at the first $k$ with
$x\in\{1,m-1\}$. Cost $O(\lambda(m)\log^2 m)$ bit operations, where $\lambda$ is
the Carmichael function; at most six multiplications for $m = 56$.

\paragraph{Type census.} Enumerate the residues coprime to $m$ and tabulate
$T$; cost $O(m\lambda(m))$ modular operations. Output $d\mapsto N(d)$ and the
densities $N(d)/\varphi(m)$.

\paragraph{Entropy and mutual information.} From a list of pairs $(x_i,t_i)$ form
empirical marginals and joint distribution and evaluate
$I = \sum \hat p(x,t)\log_2\frac{\hat p(x,t)}{\hat p(x)\hat p(t)}$ and
$H(T) = -\sum\hat p(t)\log_2\hat p(t)$; cost $O(n)$. For a deterministic channel
the two agree to machine precision, which is Theorem~\ref{thm:pin} in floating
point.

\paragraph{Frobenius orbit decomposition.} Repeatedly translate an unvisited
element by $g$ until it returns, collecting the orbit; repeat. Cost $O(|A|)$
group operations; certifies the common orbit length $f$ and the count $|A|/f$.

\paragraph{Group-order-profile entropy.} For $A = \prod_i C_{n_i}$ compute each
element's order as the lcm of coordinate orders, tabulate, apply the entropy
formula; cost $O(|A|)$.

\paragraph{Conductor selection.} For target degree $n$, enumerate $f$ with
$\varphi(f) = 2n$, compute the exponent of $(\Z/f)^\times/\{\pm1\}$, and classify
as cyclic (exponent $=n$) or not. At $n = 12$ this yields the ten conductors
$35,39,45,52,56,70,72,78,84,90$ and identifies $56$ as the smallest non-cyclic
case.

\section{Discussion}

\paragraph{Structure detection from prime statistics.} The pipeline ``count prime
types, compute entropy, compare to a table of group entropies'' is a measurement
procedure for Galois groups whose verdict is provably correct here, and which uses
only remainders---no field arithmetic.

\paragraph{Sharpness of pinning.} The vanishing gap is a theorem about
determinism, not a feature of the conductor. Its contrapositive is a diagnostic:
an empirically observed gap indicates sampling noise or a non-functional channel.

\paragraph{Coarseness.} The loss $H(X\mid T)\approx 2.855$ measures how much
coarser the type is than the residue. The efficiency
$H(T)/H(X) = (\tfrac43+\tfrac14\log_23)/(3+\log_23)\approx 0.377$ is lower than
the cyclic value $H(C_{12})/\log_224\approx 0.527$: a small exponent means fewer
distinct orders to report, so non-cyclicity costs observability.

\paragraph{Semiprimes.} Observing $pq$ instead of $p$ loses nothing at the level
of type statistics, by translation invariance. Hence such statistics cannot serve
as a primality signal, but remain fully usable on integers known only up to a
two-factor split.

\paragraph{Scope.} All results are exact statements about finite objects and about
$\Q(\zetaf)^{+}$. The passage from residue densities to densities among primes is
classical Dirichlet/Chebotarev; the finite computations are unconditional. The
entropy invariant is proved complete only at order $12$.

\section{Future directions}

\begin{conjecture}[Entropy injectivity for abelian rungs]
For abelian groups $A,B$ of the same order $n\le 100$, the order-profile
entropies agree if and only if $A\cong B$; i.e.\ the type entropy is a complete
invariant of the isomorphism class in that range.
\end{conjecture}

The key insight is that the order profile of an abelian group is the multiset
determined by $\varphi(d)$ times the number of cyclic factors admitting order $d$,
and the entropy is a $\Q$-linear combination of $\log_2 p$ over the primes
$p\mid n$, with coefficients that are $\Q$-independent unless the profiles
coincide; linear independence of $\{\log_2 p\}$ over $\Q$ then converts an
analytic coincidence into a combinatorial one. The present work shows the
invariant is not vacuous at $n = 12$, the first order admitting two abelian
groups.

\begin{conjecture}[Universal pinning for deterministic arithmetic channels]
For every modulus $m$, every $\{\pm1\}$-stable channel
$T:(\Z/m)^\times\to\N$ factoring through $(\Z/m)^\times/\{\pm1\}$ satisfies
$I = H(T)$ and $H(T)\le \log_2(\#\text{divisors of the exponent})$, with equality
if and only if $T$ is the order function of an elementary abelian group.
\end{conjecture}

Further directions: the orbit-purity and pair laws hold in any finite abelian
group and so transfer to every conductor; extending the entropy invariant beyond
the abelian case requires replacing the order of the Frobenius element by its
conjugacy class, with the class-profile entropy as the natural candidate; and
effective Chebotarev bounds would turn the entropic certificate into a finite,
verifiable computation over an explicit prime range.

\section{Conclusion}

At conductor $56$ the abelian ladder reaches its first rung whose Galois group is
of composite order and not cyclic. We have shown that $\Q(\zetaf)^{+}$ has degree
$12$ with Galois group $C_6\times C_2$; that the splitting type of a prime is the
order of its Frobenius class, with densities
$\tfrac1{12},\tfrac14,\tfrac16,\tfrac12$; that the residue-to-type channel is
deterministic and hence fully pinned, transmitting exactly
$\tfrac43+\tfrac14\log_23 = 1.7296$ bits with zero gap and strictly positive
residual uncertainty; that all Frobenius orbits are pure, giving $f\cdot g = 12$
with four shapes; that semiprimes reproduce the prime profile exactly; and that
this single measured number lies below the threshold $2$, which no cyclic group of
order $12$ can achieve. A quantity computed from prime remainders thereby
determines the isomorphism class of a Galois group.

\end{document}

